WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:07.931
We begin by adopting the lumped circuit
abstraction, which treats components as

2
00:00:07.931 --> 00:00:13.841
localized entities where internal
electromagnetic wave propagation is negligible

3
00:00:13.841 --> 00:00:17.125
compared to signal travel times.

4
00:00:17.125 --> 00:00:26.922
This assumption allows us to model circuits as
interconnected nodes and branches rather than

5
00:00:26.922 --> 00:00:28.321
distributed fields.

6
00:00:28.321 --> 00:00:36.118
Imagine a simple battery connected to a
resistor through short wires; the voltage

7
00:00:36.118 --> 00:00:42.715
appears instantaneously across the resistor
because the physical dimensions are tiny

8
00:00:42.715 --> 00:00:45.714
relative to the signal wavelength.

9
00:00:45.714 --> 00:00:52.891
This abstraction holds when the circuit size
is much smaller than the signal's wavelength,

10
00:00:52.891 --> 00:00:58.017
ensuring that voltage and current are
well-defined at each node.

11
00:00:58.017 --> 00:01:03.500
We will consistently use this model throughout
our analysis.

12
00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:11.821
Next, we establish consistent reference
polarities and current directions for every

13
00:01:11.821 --> 00:01:12.564
element.

14
00:01:12.564 --> 00:01:20.921
This systematic approach prevents sign errors
and ensures our equations reflect physical

15
00:01:20.921 --> 00:01:21.617
reality.

16
00:01:21.617 --> 00:01:30.087
For example, if we assign the positive
terminal of a battery to the top node and

17
00:01:30.087 --> 00:01:36.440
define current flowing clockwise through the
resistor, we must maintain these conventions

18
00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:38.028
throughout the analysis.

19
00:01:38.028 --> 00:01:45.278
The direction we choose is arbitrary, but
consistency is critical.

20
00:01:45.278 --> 00:01:53.153
When we later write equations, a positive
voltage drop across a resistor means current

21
00:01:53.153 --> 00:02:01.028
flows from the higher to lower potential,
while a negative value indicates the actual

22
00:02:01.028 --> 00:02:03.278
direction opposes our assumption.

23
00:02:03.278 --> 00:02:11.350
This disciplined labeling simplifies solving
the resulting algebraic system.

24
00:02:11.498 --> 00:02:18.858
With reference directions set, we apply
Kirchhoff's current and voltage laws to

25
00:02:18.858 --> 00:02:20.698
constrain the circuit.

26
00:02:20.698 --> 00:02:29.389
Kirchhoff's current law states that the sum of
currents entering any node equals zero,

27
00:02:29.389 --> 00:02:31.251
reflecting charge conservation.

28
00:02:31.251 --> 00:02:41.134
Kirchhoff's voltage law requires that the sum
of voltage drops around any closed loop equals

29
00:02:41.134 --> 00:02:43.769
zero, reflecting energy conservation.

30
00:02:43.769 --> 00:02:52.451
For our series circuit, we write one KCL
equation at the top node and one KVL equation

31
00:02:52.451 --> 00:02:53.983
around the loop.

32
00:02:53.983 --> 00:03:00.801
These constraints reduce the number of
unknowns and provide the structural framework

33
00:03:00.801 --> 00:03:03.073
for solving the circuit.

34
00:03:03.073 --> 00:03:10.950
We then combine these with element laws to
form a complete set of equations that uniquely

35
00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:13.412
determine all voltages and currents.

36
00:03:13.562 --> 00:03:22.466
We now introduce the constitutive relations
that describe how resistors and capacitors

37
00:03:22.466 --> 00:03:23.208
behave.

38
00:03:23.208 --> 00:03:31.726
A resistor obeys Ohm's law, where voltage drop
equals resistance times current.

39
00:03:31.726 --> 00:03:40.389
A capacitor relates current to the rate of
voltage change, storing electric-field energy

40
00:03:40.389 --> 00:03:44.387
as charge accumulates on its plates.

41
00:03:44.387 --> 00:03:52.669
These relations are fundamental because they
connect electrical quantities to physical

42
00:03:52.669 --> 00:03:53.422
mechanisms.

43
00:03:53.422 --> 00:04:02.739
For example, when current flows into a
capacitor, voltage rises proportionally to the

44
00:04:02.739 --> 00:04:04.172
accumulated charge.

45
00:04:04.172 --> 00:04:11.970
We use these laws alongside Kirchhoff
constraints to build the complete mathematical

46
00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:19.119
model of the circuit, ensuring every element's
behavior is accurately represented.

47
00:04:19.274 --> 00:04:28.196
When we combine a resistor and capacitor in
series, the circuit exhibits a characteristic

48
00:04:28.196 --> 00:04:33.295
time constant, tau, defined as resistance
times capacitance.

49
00:04:33.295 --> 00:04:39.027
This value determines how quickly the circuit
responds to changes.

50
00:04:39.027 --> 00:04:47.720
For example, if tau equals one second, the
capacitor voltage will reach approximately 63

51
00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:52.688
percent of its final value after one second.

52
00:04:52.688 --> 00:05:00.295
The time constant emerges naturally from
solving the differential equation that governs

53
00:05:00.295 --> 00:05:01.563
the circuit.

54
00:05:01.563 --> 00:05:09.266
It represents the intrinsic speed of the
system, balancing the resistor's opposition to

55
00:05:09.266 --> 00:05:14.599
current flow with the capacitor's ability to
store charge.

56
00:05:14.599 --> 00:05:22.056
Understanding tau allows us to predict
response times without solving the full

57
00:05:22.056 --> 00:05:23.920
equation every time.

58
00:05:24.074 --> 00:05:32.666
We now analyze how the capacitor charges and
discharges when connected to a voltage source.

59
00:05:32.666 --> 00:05:40.972
During charging, current flows from the source
through the resistor into the capacitor,

60
00:05:40.972 --> 00:05:44.166
causing voltage to rise exponentially.

61
00:05:44.166 --> 00:05:52.048
The differential equation governing this
process yields a solution where voltage

62
00:05:52.048 --> 00:05:55.631
approaches the source value asymptotically.

63
00:05:55.631 --> 00:06:04.057
Discharging occurs when the source is removed
and the capacitor releases its stored energy

64
00:06:04.057 --> 00:06:05.863
through the resistor.

65
00:06:05.863 --> 00:06:14.202
The voltage decays exponentially, following
the same time constant but in reverse.

66
00:06:14.202 --> 00:06:21.776
These behaviors demonstrate how energy
transfers between the electric field and the

67
00:06:21.776 --> 00:06:29.982
resistor, with the resistor dissipating energy
as heat while the capacitor temporarily stores

68
00:06:29.982 --> 00:06:30.613
it.

69
00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:38.288
We distinguish between transient and
steady-state behavior to understand long-term

70
00:06:38.288 --> 00:06:39.791
circuit operation.

71
00:06:39.791 --> 00:06:47.787
The transient phase occurs immediately after a
change, characterized by exponential charging

72
00:06:47.787 --> 00:06:52.452
or discharging governed by the time constant.

73
00:06:52.452 --> 00:07:00.103
The steady state emerges after several time
constants, when the capacitor voltage

74
00:07:00.103 --> 00:07:03.291
stabilizes at the source value.

75
00:07:03.291 --> 00:07:10.984
Initial capacitor voltage is continuous unless
an impulse supplies unbounded current,

76
00:07:10.984 --> 00:07:15.880
ensuring no sudden jumps in stored energy.

77
00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:23.335
Final steady-state behavior can be checked
independently by replacing the capacitor with

78
00:07:23.335 --> 00:07:27.684
a short circuit, which simplifies the
analysis.

79
00:07:27.684 --> 00:07:36.230
This separation allows us to verify solutions
and predict long-term performance without

80
00:07:36.230 --> 00:07:39.791
solving the full differential equation.

81
00:07:39.938 --> 00:07:49.585
We now examine how the RC circuit functions as
a low-pass filter, attenuating high-frequency

82
00:07:49.585 --> 00:07:53.030
signals while passing low-frequency ones.

83
00:07:53.030 --> 00:08:01.078
When the input voltage changes slowly, the
capacitor has time to charge fully, allowing

84
00:08:01.078 --> 00:08:05.102
the output to follow the input closely.

85
00:08:05.102 --> 00:08:14.435
When the input changes rapidly, the capacitor
cannot charge quickly enough, so the output

86
00:08:14.435 --> 00:08:17.102
voltage lags and attenuates.

87
00:08:17.102 --> 00:08:24.870
This frequency-dependent behavior arises
because the capacitor's impedance decreases

88
00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:27.459
with increasing frequency.

89
00:08:27.459 --> 00:08:37.132
The circuit thus acts as a smooth filter,
removing rapid fluctuations while preserving

90
00:08:37.132 --> 00:08:38.620
slow variations.

91
00:08:38.620 --> 00:08:47.137
This property is essential in audio and signal
processing applications where clean, stable

92
00:08:47.137 --> 00:08:49.102
signals are required.

93
00:08:49.250 --> 00:08:59.511
We define the cutoff frequency, omega_c, as
the point where the output voltage drops to 35

94
00:08:59.511 --> 00:09:02.717
percent of its input value.

95
00:09:02.717 --> 00:09:10.703
This frequency is inversely proportional to
the time constant, calculated as one divided

96
00:09:10.703 --> 00:09:11.932
by tau.

97
00:09:11.932 --> 00:09:21.048
Signals with frequencies below omega_c pass
through with minimal attenuation, while

98
00:09:21.048 --> 00:09:26.021
signals above omega_c are significantly
reduced.

99
00:09:26.021 --> 00:09:33.660
The cutoff frequency provides a clear boundary
for the circuit's frequency response,

100
00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:38.753
indicating the highest frequency it can
effectively transmit.

101
00:09:38.753 --> 00:09:47.277
By adjusting the resistor or capacitor values,
we can shift this boundary to filter different

102
00:09:47.277 --> 00:09:48.414
signal ranges.

103
00:09:48.414 --> 00:09:56.402
This parameter is crucial for designing
filters that meet specific frequency

104
00:09:56.402 --> 00:09:57.128
requirements.

105
00:09:57.290 --> 00:10:05.847
Finally, we calculate the complete step
response and verify energy conservation.

106
00:10:05.847 --> 00:10:14.641
When a voltage step is applied, the capacitor
charges exponentially, and the resistor

107
00:10:14.641 --> 00:10:17.347
dissipates energy as heat.

108
00:10:17.347 --> 00:10:25.906
The total energy stored in the capacitor
equals the energy supplied by the source minus

109
00:10:25.906 --> 00:10:29.329
the energy dissipated in the resistor.

110
00:10:29.329 --> 00:10:38.845
Limiting cases at zero time, long time, zero
frequency, and high frequency provide useful

111
00:10:38.845 --> 00:10:40.204
error checks.

112
00:10:40.204 --> 00:10:47.406
Time-domain step response and frequency-domain
filtering are complementary descriptions of

113
00:10:47.406 --> 00:10:51.007
the same linear circuit behavior.

114
00:10:51.007 --> 00:10:59.235
By solving the differential equation and
verifying these checks, we ensure our solution

115
00:10:59.235 --> 00:11:02.400
is accurate and physically meaningful.
